```
// Read-only lookups are always allowed. authority_get_approval must be
// permitted so that its response is recorded and can serve as evidence.
@id("allow_readonly_lookups")
permit (
  principal,
  action in [
    Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_tasks",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_costs",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_actuals_vs_planned",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_kpis",
    Drupe::Action::"get_failure_codes",
    Drupe::Action::"get_my_assigned_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"get_schedule_calendar",
    Drupe::Action::"list_workorders"
  ],
  resource
);

// Closing is allowed only at MAIN, and only if, within the last hour in this
// session, a completed authority_get_approval call for the same work order and
// site returned decision GRANTED with a HUMAN approver.
@id("close_workorder_main_with_human_approval")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"close_workorder", resource)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    eventPrincipal: principal,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    output.decision: "GRANTED",
    output.approver_kind: "HUMAN"
  }
};
```

Approving, generating, assigning, updating and cancelling work orders have no permit, so they are denied by default.

I chose `authority_get_approval` as the source of approval evidence, and its `decision` and `approver_kind` values `"GRANTED"` and `"HUMAN"` are assumed. The schema declares these two fields only as strings, so check the literals against the real tool output. `get_workorder` offers a similar but separate approval signal that I did not use.
